1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:14,592
This skill writes a plain-English status note for the project's channel: what is running, what finished, what is blocked and on whom. The facts come from the computer itself, not from anyone's memory.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:29,381
The skill reads the facts fresh each time, and that is what makes it special. Status reports once went wrong three ways in one day: a job was missed, a stopped build was called running, and times were guessed.

3
00:00:29,381 --> 00:00:41,713
Where the project's status document and the facts disagree, the facts win and the update says so. Add the words dry run, and it shows the note without posting it.

4
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:56,807
Behind the scenes it runs the skill's fact script, which only reads; the member typed only the skill's name. It looks at running jobs on every lab account, the log of the agents, new results and today's saved work.

5
00:00:56,807 --> 00:01:00,361
A check it cannot run says so.

6
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:15,317
Here is a real dry run. Here the job check could not run on a laptop, so the honest note says it could not tell what was running. Thirteen pieces of work were saved today, nothing is blocked, and the next step says whose it is.

7
00:01:15,317 --> 00:01:24,444
Before posting, the skill checks the note against the lab's rules. Here it stops, because the member asked for a dry run.

8
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:47,750
A message in a channel is easy to copy, so some things stay out. No passwords. No results, only what ran and when. Nothing from a publication folder. And no count under six of people or events, which could identify someone. A note that breaks a rule is fixed before anything is posted.

9
00:01:48,150 --> 00:02:11,150
To use it, type the skill's name in Claude Code from the project's folder, with dry run the first time. Each project can tailor it: which channel, which results folder, and the names to use for each person. When the update says a job stopped, the computer showed it stop.
